## 4. Architecture that must survive recovery

The design is the Unified Authority Kernel plus the implemented bounded
semantic exchange. It is still referred to as UAK in normative documents; V1
means the current production refinement/acceptance version, not a second
architecture.

### 4.1 Mathematical backbone

The model separates the authority slot, authority/capability kernel and full
protocol:

```text
U = NoAuthority | LiveAuthority(A)
Omega = (A, K)
Sigma = (U, P, D, L)
KernelStep_E(Omega, command)
Step_E(Sigma, event)
```

`A` owns every retained transaction and authoritative projection. `K` owns
linear checkout, settlement, publication and ordered-control capabilities.
`P`, `D` and `L` model boundary protocols, derived/public observations and
lifecycle state. A batch/optimization is accepted only when its observation is
equivalent to one named canonical no-interleaving fold of `KernelStep_E` from
the same initial cut.

Core conservation laws:

- every transaction occupies exactly one `OwnedTx` location or `Nowhere`;
- resource rows exist exactly for resident owners and equal the checked fold of
  their charges;
- ownership, charge, indexes, versions and committed effects change in one
  Apply;
- every current Computing owner has one move-only current capability at stable
  cuts; stale capabilities have no mutation right and retire in bounded work;
- free plus held compute capacity equals configured active work;
- retries against an unchanged authority cut/evidence epoch/source level are
  forbidden; each obligation has a finite rank or monotonic level with one
  named releaser;
- legal or hostile input, resource pressure, cancellation and external failure
  are typed ordinary outcomes, not integrity failure.

### 4.2 Production authority and lifecycle

`TxPoolAuthority.entries` is the only mutable transaction authority. Its owner
variants are mutually exclusive `PreAccepted`, `Accepted` and bounded inert
`ReplacementHistory`. PreAccepted phases encode validation/work progress;
Accepted membership owns the exact causal/conflict graph. Scheduler,
dependency, resources, query/template data and effect state are projections
inside the same authority, never second decision owners.

Validation produces typed evidence. Plan is read-only and has no external I/O.
Apply validates its version/capability and atomically commits. External I/O
consumes committed effects and cannot retroactively decide whether Apply
happened. Production Rust does not use panic/catch as normal invariant control;
valid hostile input must not stop the service.

Exact transaction identity roles remain separate. Verification cache identity
is the inline 32-byte witness transaction hash plus
`ScriptVerificationRules`; raw hash, witness hash, proposal ID, short ID and
chain view are never substituted. Same-tip positive cell evidence is reused
only with exact per-input chain provenance; pool-produced cells remain coupled
to the overlay.

### 4.3 Selected bounded semantic exchange

The machine contract has these components:

| Component | Disposition and production owner |
|---|---|
| atomic batch Apply | implemented in `authority/plan.rs`; one batch clock/Apply with distinct owner versions |
| retained ingress combiner | implemented in `service/builder.rs` and ingress Plan; same-peer/count/byte-bounded canonical prefixes |
| compute exchange | implemented in `authority/plan/compute_exchange.rs`; one settle/refill exchange per available wave |
| Ready/effect overlap | retained in `authority/plan/settlement.rs`; bounded Ready batches and claimed effect publication |
| prepared full query | implemented in `authority/query.rs`; fallible scratch outside the write cut |
| typed ordered control | implemented in `service.rs`; linear public administration and reliable chain lane |
| bounded cache writer | retained in `authority/topology.rs`; best effort, non-authoritative and nonblocking |
| concurrent template lanes | retained in `authority/template_driver.rs`; full/reset ordering without serializing proposal/transaction/uncle lanes |

Independent, chain-backed work resolves/verifies concurrently outside the
authority and shares bounded ingress, completion and Ready Apply cuts. Shared
inputs, dependency components, RBF, capacity eviction, effect pressure and
refreshed evidence use canonical or exact coupled settlement. There is one
authority in both cases; no separate DAG/shard owns lifecycle state.

Full and reset template publication remain serial through their intended
ordering boundary. Proposal, transaction and uncle/partial lanes stay
optimistically concurrent. A skipped optimistic update is repaired by the next
source/version change; the design must not serialize all template roles.

Peer ban behavior remains deliberately simple: remove only attributed
not-yet-Accepted work, settle/release relay-known handoff state, keep authority
projections clean and allow the same transaction to be learned again from
another peer. Accepted transactions are not deleted merely because their
original ingress peer is later banned.

